{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1014825-covenants-1st-vs-covenants-2nd","id":1014825,"title":"Covenants 1st Vs Covenants 2nd?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-12-09T10:27:26-08:00","id":1014825,"title":"Covenants 1st Vs Covenants 2nd?","content":"So in this past month my curiosity was picked by Requiem and started reading the Covenant book of my least favorite Covenant, the Lancea Sanctum and i quite like what i read and push them to one of my favorites.<br /> <br /> However now that Secrets of the Covenants is out and we got the full 2nd version of the covenants. Is there much difference between 1st and 2nd edition covenants? Are the covenants books from 1st edition a good buy now?<br /> <br /> I mean my 3 least favorite covenants were the Lancea Sactum, Circle of the Crone and Carthians. The lancea Sactum covenant book sold me on then not being a senseless catholic-are-bad parody. However i want to see if the Carthian can be more than &quot;Che-Guevara-tshirt-wearing-firebombers&quot; and can actually be interesting when not in power, because all the examples of interesting Carthians i ever heard were when they were in power. And the Crone which seems to me bloody wiccans. So i am looking for material that shatter those perceptions.<br /> <br /> Thanks","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T10:36:01-08:00","id":1014831,"title":"","content":"They should at least clear up those misconceptions. The covenants aren't that different, but it's presented in vastly different way.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T12:46:19-08:00","id":1014894,"title":"","content":"I wouldn't look to Secrets of the Covenants to be the &quot;full 2e version,&quot; the book is presented far more like that 1e Clan books: a huge amount of in-character writing to get a feel for the attitudes of them from the inside, as well as examinations of different iterations of them in different places. There's a lot of cool ideas in there that might help sell you on the Covenants you don't like, but it's very different from the 1e Covenant books; which in a way keeps the 1e ones more relevant than replaced.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T12:58:30-08:00","id":1014902,"title":"","content":"That's true. The first edition books are much more informative. I found them really dense, but it's all basically about the covenants and how they appear in different areas, ranking systems and their histories.<br /> <br /> Secrets of the Covenants seems to try to reinforce that you are not a stereotype just because you're a member of a covenant, and show some 'new' developments you can incorporate into plots relating to the covenants. Some seem to show how the powers, like the Cruac styles, work in-character.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T17:12:41-08:00","id":1015016,"title":"","content":"Honestly the Covenants haven't changed that much from 1E to 2E. Primarily, they've been finely polished to better illustrate the stories and gameplay the Covenants specialize in. The best write ups for them in my opinion are as presented in Requiem 2nd Edition Core, becaus they use the best of the Covenant Books but condense and refine it. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I would still greatly recommend Secrets of the Covenants though (and the other Covenant books for that matter) because more than anything else it give us an in-game look at members of the Covenants, from the best to the worst.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","user":"Dusksage","date":".  Polish 12-09-2016, 08:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}